WEBVTT

cue-VID_U3_03-1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:33.000
Meet Auralis Audio, a premium headphone maker launching the Nimbus One. Four tiles orbit it: Product, Price, Place and Promotion. Marketing mix means all the activities which go into marketing a product or service, often summarised as the four Ps. The plan is twenty thousand units at a one hundred and twenty dollar price, sold online and in shops. Every tile must support the others. Follow the life cycle, pricing, distribution and two kinds of promotion in one journey.

cue-VID_U3_03-2
00:00:33.000 --> 00:01:05.000
Product comes first. Brand image is an image or identity given to a product which distinguishes it from competitors brands. Packaging is the physical container or wrapping to protect a product and help in promoting it. The case opens to reveal a matte finish and a brand badge, signalling quality on the shelf. New products can win fresh customers, yet they consume cash and can fail in a crowded market. Good design must serve the chosen buyer.

cue-VID_U3_03-3
00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:36.000
The product life cycle shows the stages a product passes through: introduction, growth, maturity and decline. A curve draws in. Sales climb from two thousand units in introduction, to nine thousand in growth, to fourteen thousand at maturity, then ease to six thousand in decline. The line rises, flattens, then falls. Each stage needs a different mix: launch spending early, differentiation at maturity, cost control late. Reading the curve tells managers when to act.

cue-VID_U3_03-4
00:01:36.000 --> 00:02:06.000
At maturity, extension strategies can lift the curve. Four tiles appear: new markets, new uses, adapting the product or packaging, and increased advertising plus sales promotion. Auralis Audio tries a sports version and a gift pack, and the curve bends upward twice. New markets bring buyers but need research. New uses widen appeal but may confuse the brand. Adapting packaging is cheap but short lived. Choose the extension that fits the segment.

cue-VID_U3_03-5
00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:36.000
Cost plus pricing is the cost of manufacturing the product plus a profit mark up. Competitive pricing sets the product in line with rivals to protect market share. Dynamic pricing changes the price online as demand shifts; the tag flickers with a demand meter. A loss leader sells one item cheaply to draw shoppers into the range. Revenue direction lines rise or dip with each method, and no formula is needed here.

cue-VID_U3_03-6
00:02:36.000 --> 00:03:06.000
Two launch prices. Penetration pricing sets a price below rivals to enter a market and gain market share; the low line climbs as buyers join. Price skimming sets a high price for a new product; the high line starts high and eases as buyers are served. When price is lower, more buyers may respond, so revenue can grow. Because the Nimbus One is premium, therefore skimming recovers costs first, so promotion can follow.

cue-VID_U3_03-7
00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:38.000
Place is the means by which a product is passed from the producer to the customer. Direct sales run from Auralis Audio straight to buyers online. Indirect routes pass through a wholesaler, then a retailer, then the customer, with an agent. Product dots travel both paths. Ecommerce is the online buying and selling of goods and services using internet linked devices. It widens reach but adds delivery, returns and security costs. Direct keeps control; indirect widens coverage.

cue-VID_U3_03-8
00:03:38.000 --> 00:04:09.000
Promotion has two parts. Advertising is a paid for communication with potential customers about a product to encourage them to buy it. Informative advertising explains features; persuasive advertising builds feeling and status. Social media, emails, leaflets and billboards show both. Sales promotions are incentives such as special offers, discounts, or reward schemes aimed at consumers to achieve short term increases in sales; vouchers join them. Information builds understanding, persuasion lifts desire, offers trigger action.

cue-VID_U3_03-9
00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:38.000
Now judge the whole mix. A premium product with skimming, selective retail and informative promotion supports a high price. The benefit is margin and image; the limit is lower volume and higher selling costs. A penetration price may grow share but weaken the premium story. Because the four Ps must fit together, therefore Auralis Audio keeps the premium set, so every tile reinforces one message. A coherent mix beats a mismatch.

